  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nA heavy rainfall event is coming into better focus for much of \nsouthwest and west central Kansas, as the North American Monsoon \ncirculation remains shunted farther east of its climatological \nposition with the upper level high centered over Arkansas. At the \nsurface, early afternoon observations were showing widespread \ndewpoints in the mid to upper 60s across all of southwest and west \ncentral Kansas. Observations and clear air radar returns show a \nnebulous mesolow circulation between DDC and GCK. A surface thermal \nridge was nosing northeast into the Red Hills region with \ntemperatures approaching 100F at Medicine Lodge on a south to \nsouthwest wind. Initial surface-based thunderstorm development is \nlikely in the 21-23Z time frame in vicinity of the mesolow and/or \nthe convergence axis near the hottest surface temperatures nearest \nthe Oklahoma line south of DDC. \n\nThe initial storms which develop within the hotter air mass \nsouth/southeast of DDC will likely be more isolated or widely \nscattered in nature, but the strongest storms will be capable of \nsevere downburst winds given the hot temperatures. Focus will then \nturn farther west/southwest as a much more organized cluster of \nstrong to severe storms develop and mature across far southeastern \nColorado and adjacent far southwestern Kansas. All reliable short-\nterm, high-resolution CAMs and associated ensemble systems (namely \nthe HREF) show an impressive signal for a healthy mesoscale \nconvective system (MCS) tracking northeast within the lower \ntropospheric Theta-E ridge. Latest NBM shows precipitable water \nvalues of 1.60 to 1.75\" across much of west central and southwest \nKansas, which is around the 95th percentile for southwest Kansas \nthis time of year. The above-mentioned meteorological setting along \nwith HREF LPMM QPF values showing max potential 2-3\" rainfall, HRRR \nrun-to-run consistency of widely scattered 1.5 to 3\" rainfall \namounts, and NBM 90th percentile QPF in the 2-3\" range all support \npotential for flash flooding with the strongest, slower-moving \nstorms. The best corridor for this activity among all the models is \nroughly 50 miles either side of a line extending from Johnson to \nGarden City to Dighton to Ellis. We have decided to issue a Flash \nFlood Watch for these counties after collaboration with the NWS \nPueblo and NWS Goodland offices. \n\nTonight's heavy rainfall event will move on into northern/central \nKansas prior to sunrise Sunday. The effective frontal zone will push \nfairly far to the south thanks to all the outflow from the MCS with \na light northeasterly wind prevailing across southwest Kansas. This \nshould knock afternoon temperatures down 7 to 10 degrees over much \nof our southwest Kansas region with this \"cooler\", more moist air \nmass hanging around through Monday. After Monday, the temperature \nforecast becomes a little more challenging as a hot, downslope \nsouthwest wind air mass redevelops thanks to a new surface low \nforming over Nebraska. However, the magnitude of continued \nevapotranspiration from all the recent rainfall may limit surface \nheating potential -- otherwise, we would likely widespread highs \naround 105 degrees again on Tuesday. Tuesday would most likely be \nthe best chance for widespread 100s, as another polar front moves \nsouth into Kansas thanks to a fairly decent west-northwest flow \nshortwave trough moving across Nebraska into Iowa. This polar front \nwill offer another increased chance for organized thunderstorm \nactivity Wednesday evening/night.",
